common --enable_bzlmod
common --noenable_workspace
test --test_output=errors

# Undeclared test outputs as one downloadable `outputs.zip` rather than
# a loose directory tree.
#
# `//datalib/ui:e2e_test` writes a Playwright HTML report — video and
# trace per test, with the trace viewer bundled — into
# TEST_UNDECLARED_OUTPUTS_DIR. Zipped, it arrives as a single artifact
# on the BuildBuddy invocation page that you can download and open;
# unzipped it is ~200 loose files, which is both harder to find and
# what some BES consumers decline to list at all. Nothing else in the
# tree writes undeclared outputs, so this costs one zip of one
# directory on one test.
test --zip_undeclared_test_outputs=true

# Every bazel action inherits a fixed PATH, not the developer's host
# PATH. Build and test actions resolve `npx`, `cmake`, etc. against
# this PATH, so those tools must be installed somewhere here
# (`/opt/homebrew/bin` covers the brew defaults on arm macs;
# `/usr/local/bin` covers Intel macs — brew's prefix there — plus
# Linux tools installed outside the system dirs; `/usr/bin:/bin`
# covers system `make`/`perl`/`cc` etc.). boring-sys2's build script
# in particular shells out to cmake + a C toolchain to build
# boringssl — the release workflow's x86_64-apple-darwin leg failed
# until /usr/local/bin was added here, because that's where the
# macos-15-intel runner's cmake lives.
#
# We deliberately do NOT `--action_env=NPX_BIN`. Forwarded host env
# vars contribute to every action's cache key, so any caller-side
# variance (direnv loaded vs not, `which npx` resolving to different
# paths in different shells, agent sessions, CI machines) invalidates
# every cached action — including the expensive boring-sys2 build
# script. Rust call sites still honor `$NPX_BIN` if set at *runtime*
# (handy when running the sync binary directly outside bazel), but
# bazel doesn't propagate it into the action graph.
# Resolver:
#   * NPX_BIN  — datalib_etl::latchkey::latchkey_command (the manual
#                `*_live` tests). Nothing qmd-shaped reaches npx under
#                bazel any more: both consumers stage a
#                DATALIB_RUNTIME_DIR from Bazel-managed inputs, so
#                `bundled_command` wins before the npx fallback — the
#                fixture genrule (//tests/fixtures:ingested_tng_qmd) and
#                //datalib/ui:e2e_test (see run_e2e.sh).
#
# That second one matters because of the PATH pinned just below. The
# e2e test `env_inherit`s PATH, so it ran under the developer's `node`
# while every other action ran under this line's — and npm keys its npx
# cache on the package spec alone, no Node version in it. Two Nodes,
# one directory, an ABI-specific better-sqlite3 inside it: whichever
# installed first won and the other died on NODE_MODULE_VERSION. The
# staged runtime removes the second Node; `npx_cache_dir` in
# core/src/node_runtime.rs covers the paths bazel never sees.
build --action_env=PATH=/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
build --host_action_env=PATH=/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
test --test_env=PATH=/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin

# There used to be a `--sandbox_add_mount_pair=$(HOME)/.cache/qmd/models`
# here, so the qmd-indexer genrule could read the host's ~2 GB GGUF cache
# from inside the sandbox instead of re-downloading it. Nothing in the
# build reads that directory any more: the models are pinned in
# MODULE.bazel (`@qmd_model_*`) and reach the two places that need them —
# `//tests/fixtures:ingested_tng_qmd` and `:materialize_tng_root` — as
# ordinary bazel inputs. Dropping the mount also drops its onboarding
# cost, which was a `mkdir -p ~/.cache/qmd/models` that a missing
# directory turned into a build failure rather than a warning.
#
# The host cache still exists and still matters — it is where the SHIPPED
# app puts models at sync time, which is why
# `qmd_indexer::default_models_dir()` still has to agree with qmd's own
# `MODEL_CACHE_DIR`. That agreement is asserted by the rust unit test
# `default_models_dir_matches_qmd_default` and by
# `//tools:qmd_model_cache_path_test`.
#
# `--action_env=HOME` stays. It was introduced alongside the mount, but
# it is not specific to it, and the actions that would notice its
# absence have not been enumerated.
build --action_env=HOME

# libsqlite3-sys's build script invokes bindgen, which uses
# `clang-sys` to dlopen libclang and parse <sqlite3.h>. Point it at
# Xcode Command Line Tools' bundled libclang.dylib — present on every
# macOS host (incl. GitHub Actions `macos-*` runners) since bazel
# already requires CLT for the C toolchain.
#
# `--host_action_env` mirrors `--action_env` so the *exec*-config copy
# of every bindgen build script finds libclang too. boring-sys2 (and
# other `wreq`/BoringSSL users) gets built `[for tool]` whenever an
# exec-config build tool in the `//...` graph depends on the HTTP
# stack; without the host variant that exec-config bindgen panics with
# "Unable to find libclang" the moment the action isn't a cache hit —
# which is exactly what reddened `bazel test //...` in CI on a cold
# BuildBuddy cache. `--action_env` alone only covers target-config
# actions, so the failure hid behind the remote cache until a miss.
#
# Linux hosts must override both on the command line (later flags win):
#   bazel build ... --action_env=LIBCLANG_PATH=$(llvm-config --libdir) \
#                   --host_action_env=LIBCLANG_PATH=$(llvm-config --libdir)
# `release.yml` does this for the linux-amd64 / linux-arm64 matrix
# entries. The devcontainer's user-level ~/.bazelrc carries the same
# override so interactive Linux work doesn't need to remember the flag.
#
# DO NOT set LIBCLANG_PATH via `crate.annotation(... build_script_env)`
# in MODULE.bazel — that wins over `--action_env` and would re-break
# Linux. See the libsqlite3-sys annotation comment.
build --action_env=LIBCLANG_PATH=/Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/lib
build --host_action_env=LIBCLANG_PATH=/Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/lib

# Workspace status — tools/workspace_status.sh emits STABLE_GIT_HASH +
# STABLE_GIT_DESCRIBE. Always run so any `--config=release` invocation
# has fresh values; the script itself is sub-second.
build --workspace_status_command=tools/workspace_status.sh

# Stamping (`--stamp`) is OFF by default and ON only for release
# builds (`--config=release`).
#
# When `--stamp` is on, every action that consumes workspace status —
# in this repo, just `datalib_sync_bin`'s rustc_env_files
# substitution of {STABLE_GIT_HASH} / {STABLE_GIT_DESCRIBE} — picks
# up the new git SHA on every commit, which invalidates its action
# cache key. Result: every commit forced a rebuild of the sync
# binary even when no rust changed.
#
# We don't care about a stamped binary for day-to-day work — the
# `datalib-sync --version` output reads "unknown" off the
# `option_env!` fallback in version.rs, which is fine for dev. When
# building a release artifact, pass `--config=release` to opt back in.
build:release --stamp

# Shared content-addressed cache across checkouts. Bazel's action cache lives
# in the per-workspace output base (`_bazel_<user>/<md5-of-workspace-path>/`),
# so two checkouts at different paths (e.g. Dropbox tree vs Sculptor clone)
# don't share hits. The disk cache is keyed by action hash and shared.
build --disk_cache=~/Library/Caches/bazel-disk-cache
test  --disk_cache=~/Library/Caches/bazel-disk-cache

# BuildBuddy remote cache + build event service. Opt-in via
# `--config=buildbuddy` on `build` / `test`; the API key lives in
# `.bazelrc.user` (gitignored) — see the `try-import` at the bottom of
# this file. Without the user file the config is inert; with it,
# subsequent invocations both read from and write to a shared content-
# addressed cache hosted at imbue.buildbuddy.io (our org-scoped
# endpoint), and post Build Event Protocol events to the same host for
# inspection.
#
# Why this is huge for our build: actions that anyone (you, CI, the
# devcontainer warmup, a contributor) builds once become cache hits
# for everyone else. boring-sys2's BoringSSL compile, rules_rust's
# crate compiles, and the bzlmod-resolved external repos all flow
# through, so first-build cold cost on a fresh machine drops by ~90%.
#
# `--remote_timeout=600` because the doltlite amalgamation archive is
# fetched through the cache layer and the default 60s is tight for
# multi-MB uploads on residential uplinks.
#
# `--remote_upload_local_results=true` is the default but we name it
# explicitly so it's obvious this is a read+write cache, not a
# read-only mirror. Untrusted CI contexts (PR builds from forks) should
# pass `--remote_upload_local_results=false` to avoid poisoning the
# cache with unverified outputs.
build:buildbuddy --bes_results_url=https://imbue.buildbuddy.io/invocation/
build:buildbuddy --bes_backend=grpcs://imbue.buildbuddy.io
build:buildbuddy --remote_cache=grpcs://imbue.buildbuddy.io
build:buildbuddy --remote_timeout=600
build:buildbuddy --remote_upload_local_results=true

# Fetch repository downloads through BuildBuddy too, not just action
# results. Bazel asks BuildBuddy for the URL; BuildBuddy serves it from
# its cache if it has it, otherwise fetches it once from the origin and
# keeps it. So `http_archive` / `http_file` content — the rust
# toolchains, the doltlite amalgamation, the qmd GGUFs — comes from the
# same host as everything else instead of from a third-party server per
# machine, per CI container.
#
# The qmd models are why this is here. They are ~2.26 GB of
# `@qmd_model_*` (MODULE.bazel), a CI container starts with an empty
# repository cache, and HuggingFace answered one of those cold fetches
# with `429 Too Many Requests` on 2026-09-03 and failed the build.
#
# Measured, not assumed: fetching `@qmd_model_reranker//file` cold with
# this flag uploaded 639,174,366 bytes to BuildBuddy on the first run
# (origin fetch, then stored) and 20,814 bytes on the second, with the
# same 639,153,184 downloaded — i.e. the second fetch came from
# BuildBuddy, not HuggingFace.
#
# `_local_fallback=true` because the default is `false`, which turns a
# BuildBuddy outage into a failed fetch rather than a slow one. With it,
# bazel just downloads from the origin URL itself.
build:buildbuddy --experimental_remote_downloader=grpcs://imbue.buildbuddy.io
build:buildbuddy --experimental_remote_downloader_local_fallback=true

# Fully-static musl release builds. Selects the musl platform for the
# TARGET configuration only: build scripts, proc macros, and genrule
# tools keep running under the host (gnu/darwin) toolchains, so these
# configs are safe to combine with any build. Used by release.yml's
# `*-unknown-linux-musl` matrix legs:
#
#     bazelisk build //datalib/backend:dist -c opt --config=musl-x86_64
#
# MODULE.bazel registers the same musl target toolchains for Apple
# Silicon hosts, so much of the release path can be debugged locally
# (e.g. `datalib_http_bin` cross-builds to a fully-static ELF from
# a mac). The known exception: anything that pulls boring-sys2 (wreq —
# so `latchkey_curl_impersonate` and `datalib_sync_bin`) only musl-builds
# with a Linux exec platform (the CI runners, the devcontainer),
# because boring-sys2's build script runs bindgen against the musl
# target triple, which needs linux libc headers on the include path;
# a linux host supplies them via /usr/include, a mac has none for a
# linux triple and bindgen dies on `stddef.h`. The `bazel build ...
# (musl static)` step in test.yml gates the full musl dist on every
# PR, so darwin's partial coverage is fine.
#
# The resulting binaries are statically linked against musl libc (no
# DT_NEEDED entries at all — verify with `file`, which should say
# "statically linked" / "static-pie linked"), so they run on any Linux
# of the right arch regardless of libc.
build:musl-x86_64 --platforms=//platforms:linux_x86_64_musl
build:musl-aarch64 --platforms=//platforms:linux_aarch64_musl

# `.bazelrc.user` is per-user (gitignored). Today it carries the
# BuildBuddy `--remote_header=x-buildbuddy-api-key=...` line and an
# `import %workspace%/.bazelrc` line followed by `build --config=buildbuddy`
# (or callers add `--config=buildbuddy` themselves). Template:
#
#     common --remote_header=x-buildbuddy-api-key=<your-api-key>
#     build --config=buildbuddy
#     test  --config=buildbuddy
#
# Get your key at https://imbue.buildbuddy.io/settings.
try-import %workspace%/.bazelrc.user

# `--config=ci` — turn silent staleness into a build failure. Applied by
# both jobs in `test.yml`; NOT applied locally on purpose (see below).
#
# `MODULE.bazel.lock` records content hashes of its resolution inputs,
# including `datalib/backend/Cargo.{toml,lock}`. Bumping the workspace
# version invalidates them, and bazel's DEFAULT `--lockfile_mode=update`
# then re-resolves and rewrites the file silently — never failing, never
# printing anything a releaser would notice. The result is that the lock
# only ever gets refreshed by accident:
#
#   fixed incidentally: 07282e94 (a toolchains_musl change),
#                       73ae15e6 (a crate deletion)
#   skipped by the bumps that actually invalidated it:
#                       71c315d4, 786b628f, 75abc2a7, d5f2aa47
#
# Four consecutive release bumps shipped stale, and CI stayed green
# throughout, because "silently re-resolve" is indistinguishable from
# "nothing to do". `error` makes bazel refuse instead, naming the
# extension and the offending input file:
#
#   ERROR: MODULE.bazel.lock is no longer up-to-date because an input to
#   the extension '@@rules_rust+//crate_universe:extensions.bzl%crate'
#   changed: file info or contents of @@//datalib/backend/Cargo.lock
#   changed.
#
# It also stops real work being repeated: with a stale lock bazel cannot
# replay the recorded extension results, so it re-runs the crate_universe
# extension on every cold server — i.e. every CI run.
#
# Deliberately CI-only. Locally `update` is the friendlier default: you
# change a dep, bazel fixes the lock, you commit it. Forcing `error` on
# developers would fail the build before they had any chance to
# regenerate, and the fix would be "run bazel again" — pure friction.
# `test.yml` runs on `pull_request` AND `push: main`, so both routes into
# main are covered, including release bumps that are pushed straight to
# main without a PR.
#
# When this fails, regenerate without the config and commit the result:
#
#     bazel build --nobuild //...                 # note: no --config=ci
#     git status --porcelain MODULE.bazel.lock    # expect it staged/committed
#
# One pass is enough for a plain dependency change. After a *version
# bump* it is not — measured, not guessed: the crate_universe extension
# also rewrites `datalib/backend/Cargo.lock` on pass 1, which
# invalidates the hash it recorded in the same pass, so `--config=ci`
# still fails afterwards. Pass 2 converges; pass 3 is a no-op. For
# release bumps prefer the release skill's order instead
# (`tools/repin_cargo.sh` settles Cargo.lock up front), which avoids the
# two-pass dance entirely.
#
# Note that a failed `--config=ci` run does NOT rewrite the lock — it
# aborts during module resolution — so a red CI job never leaves a
# half-updated file behind.
common:ci --lockfile_mode=error

# Auto-apply per-OS `build:<os>` configs below based on the build host.
common --enable_platform_specific_config

# Drop dead DT_NEEDED entries from Linux release binaries. libsqlite3-sys's
# build script unconditionally emits `cargo:rustc-link-lib=sqlite3` (no
# `kind=` prefix → defaults to dylib) even when its `bundled` feature is
# on and our `//third-party/doltlite:sqlite3` annotation has already
# statically linked doltlite. Without --as-needed, the linker still
# records `libsqlite3.so` as a DT_NEEDED entry on the final binary, and
# glibc's loader then refuses to launch on hosts without a system
# libsqlite3 — even though no sqlite3_* symbols are actually unresolved
# (verify with `nm -D --undefined-only datalib-sync | grep sqlite3`
# → empty). `--as-needed` tells ld to omit DT_NEEDED entries whose
# library contributes no symbols to the final image.
#
# macOS ld64 doesn't accept `-Wl,…` flags in this form and dead-strips
# unused dylibs by default anyway, so this is Linux-only. The flag is
# safe to apply to all Linux Rust links — at worst it's a no-op.
#
# `--host_linkopt` is the exec-config sibling. Bazel's exec config has
# its own flag-application surface, and `--linkopt` alone doesn't
# reach Rust binaries built `[for tool]` (the binaries genrules shell
# out to). Without the host variant, exec-config `datalib_sync_bin`
# still has the phantom `libsqlite3.so` DT_NEEDED and refuses to launch
# inside the devcontainer's slim base image during
# `//tests/fixtures:ingested_tng`.
build:linux --linkopt=-Wl,--as-needed
build:linux --host_linkopt=-Wl,--as-needed

# rustfmt-check is **always on** for every bazel build/test. This
# forbids `bazelisk test //...` from running against unformatted code,
# which would otherwise mean: developer runs tests → fix-up `cargo fmt`
# at commit time → bazel action cache invalidates → 30-40s of recompile
# on every commit. With the aspect always active, `bazelisk test //...`
# fails fast if any file is misformatted; dev runs `cargo fmt`; reruns
# tests; the test compiles the final formatted bytes; commit hits cache.
#
# rustfmt check is a fast per-crate action; the cold-cache cost is
# small and goes entirely to BuildBuddy's remote cache on shared work.
build --aspects=@rules_rust//rust:defs.bzl%rustfmt_aspect
build --output_groups=+rustfmt_checks

# Clippy, like rustfmt above, is **always on** for every bazel
# build/test. Replaces `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features --
# -D warnings` in scripts/pre-commit because the cargo invocation has
# its own incremental cache (separate from bazel's) and went cold
# after every bazel build, eating 1–2 minutes per commit. The aspect
# shares bazel's action cache + BuildBuddy remote cache.
#
# It used to be opt-in via `--config=clippy`, on the theory that clippy
# is noisier than rustfmt and belonged at commit time rather than every
# test run. That backfired twice over:
#
#   * Nothing on the default path ran it, so findings accumulated
#     silently. By the time anyone looked there were 21 across 32
#     targets, dating back to July, and `bazel run //:precommit` had
#     been red for months (see the commit that cleared them).
#   * Opt-in means ALTERNATING, and alternating is what actually costs.
#     Flipping between `--config=clippy` and a bare build changes the
#     configuration and discards the analysis cache each way. Measured
#     on this repo: a warm no-op `bazel build //...` right after a
#     clippy build took 2.4s (re-analysis) versus 0.5s once the
#     configuration stopped flipping.
#
# The aspect itself is close to free on an incremental build — clippy
# actions run alongside the rustc compiles rather than extending the
# critical path. Measured on a leaf-crate edit rebuilding `//...`:
# 14.5s without the aspect, 12.6s with it. The real cost is a cold
# cache on a first build, which BuildBuddy shares across everyone.
#
# `--config=clippy` is kept below as a no-op alias so existing
# invocations, scripts and docs keep working.
build --aspects=@rules_rust//rust:defs.bzl%rust_clippy_aspect
build --output_groups=+clippy_checks

# Back-compat alias. Deliberately does NOT re-add `--aspects`: bazel
# would then apply the same aspect twice. Re-stating the output group is
# harmless (they dedupe), and keeps `--config=clippy` a valid config
# name — bazel errors on a `--config` that no .bazelrc line defines.
build:clippy --output_groups=+clippy_checks

# Clippy *configuration* (the .toml file + flags), also on plain
# `build`. These are Starlark build settings that ride with the
# analysis cache, so they have to hold the same value on every command;
# when they lived under `build:clippy` alone, every alternation between
# a clippy build and a bare `bazel test //...` flipped them and
# discarded the analysis cache. That reasoning is what eventually
# argued for moving the aspect activation itself to plain `build` too.
#
# Auto-discover via the workspace clippy.toml. clippy is picky about
# the filename (must be `clippy.toml` or `.clippy.toml`); ours lives
# at datalib/backend/clippy.toml and is exported via that
# package's BUILD.bazel.
build --@rules_rust//rust/settings:clippy.toml=//datalib/backend:clippy.toml
# Warnings-as-errors, matching `-D warnings` from the old cargo
# invocation. `disallowed_macros` was the lint clippy.toml's
# `disallowed-macros` table relied on being warned (then promoted to
# error by `-D warnings`) — same here.
build --@rules_rust//:clippy_flags=-Dwarnings,-Wclippy::disallowed_macros
